"D-8" The torch of the 100th National Sports Festival in Dokdo has burned = South Korea (Chuo Nippo English version) - Yahoo! News
In Dokdo, the easternmost part of the Republic of Korea (Dokto, the Japanese name, Takeshima), the 100th National Sports Festival (the national standard) was burned up.
On the 26th, at 6:30am, the city of Seoul held a torchlight event commemorating the National Sports Festival at the landing site of the Dokdo National Guard in Ulleung-gun, Gyeongsangbuk-do.
It is the first time in fifteen years that the torch of the National Sports Festival in Dokdo has been ignited since the 85th National Sports Festival in Chungbuk.
Following the kite-flying performance to pray for peace, Kim Seong-hwan, vice-president of the Seoul City Council, read out the declaration.
"One hundred years ago, our people wanted to add a hundred-year history of the National Sports Festival over the history of the three-year-old movement and the establishment of the government of the Republic of Korea," Kim said, adding, "One hundred years of national sports will be added to the history of the National Sports Festival in order to honor the will of self-determination of the national sports tournament."
A greeting from the head of the county of Ulleung also continued.
“Recently, in Japan for no reason, Dokdo claims that Dokdo is its own territory,” Kim Byung-soo said, stressing that “all citizens should unite and overcome it through this National Sports Festival.”
Vice-president Kim and the head of the county both ignited the torch.
The torch was handed over to Ho Wong-seok, the first runner of Dokdo National Guard, and members of Oh Yosef, through the hands of Cho Seong-ho, head of the sports promotion division in Seoul.
The Dokdo torchfire event was scheduled for the 21st, but it was postponed to this day on the north side of Typhoon Hagibis.
"It's lonely and difficult, but we're working with pride that we can't protect our territory," Ho Won-seok, who has worked in Dokdo since January of this year, said, "It's an honor to be able to participate as a runner in the torch relay on the 100th anniversary of Dokdo."
From the Helicoputer landing site to the middle of Dokdo, and from the halfway point to the dock.
The torch moved to Ulleungdo Island and moved Kim Seung-ho, a resident of Ulleung County, to Seoul by relay.
On the 22nd, the torch began on the 22nd at Chamsondan on Gangwhae Island.
Because it is the 100th time, the torch relay will also be held on the largest scale in history.
The flames of the Mani-san (Manisan) platform will go around Incheon, Goyang, and Suwon.
The torch of Ganghua Island will burn up as a single pyrotechnic after combining the special ignitions held in Rinjin Pavilion, Mara Island (Marado) and Dokdo on the 29th of this month at Seoul Square.
On the other hand, the mayor of Seoul, Park Won-soon, said in a media briefing on the 25th that "the power of collective intelligence is important, not a few heroes now," and "Therefore, the basic concept of the National Sports Festival has also been decided as "many stars".
South Korea-Bulgaria summit discussion on ways to expand cooperation on nuclear power plants and defense industries (Yonhap News) - Yahoo! News
SEOUL (Reuters) - South Korean President Moon Jae-in met with Bulgaria's Prime Minister Borisov on Monday during an official visit to South Korea at the Cheongwadae (president's office) to discuss substantive ways to expand cooperation between the two countries.
For the first time, the Bulgarian Prime Minister's visit to South Korea.
President Moon welcomed his visit to South Korea ahead of the 30th anniversary of the establishment of diplomatic relations next year, and highly appreciated the leadership of Prime Minister Borisov, who leads Bulgaria's development.
The two leaders appreciated the fact that trade and investment between the two countries continues to increase amid the growing global protectionism, and agreed to work together to expand economic cooperation in the future.
Moon welcomed Bulgaria's policy of granting a "strategic investor" status to South Korean companies, and he said he hoped it would promote investment in Bulgaria for South Korean companies based on this.
He also stressed that South Korea could be the best partner of Bulgaria as Bulgaria promoted the introduction of nuclear power and the modernization of national defense, and called for interest and cooperation so that South Korean companies can participate in ordering nuclear power plants by the Bulgarian government and promoting the defense industry.
In addition, the two leaders agreed to strengthen future-oriented cooperation in the fields of electric vehicle (EV) batteries and auto parts and renewable energy.
Prime Minister Borisov hoped that close cooperation would also take place in the fields of information and communication technology (ICT), which South Korea leads, science and technology, and education.
The peace process on the Korean Peninsula was also discussed, and President Moon expressed gratitude for Bulgaria's support of the South Korean government's efforts to consolidate peace on the Korean Peninsula, such as making a statement in support of the North-South and U.S.-North Korea summit.
Prime Minister Borisov appreciated President Moon’s will to build a peace regime on the Korean Peninsula and the South Korean government’s efforts, and reiterated the Bulgarian government’s firm support.
On this day's meeting was also attended by Secretary of the Ministry of Industry, Trade, Industry and Energy Seong-moo, Minister of Industry, Trade and Energy, Lee Ho-seong-sung (President's Office), Chief Secretary of the Economic Affairs Office of Cheong-wadae (president's office), and the second deputy director of the National Security Office of Kim Hyun-jung.
On behalf of Mr. Kang Kyung-hwa, Secretary of the Ministry of Foreign Affairs during his visit to the U.S., Mr. Cho Se-young, First Secretary of the Ministry of Foreign Affairs, also attended.
Kinki Ceramics, the local industry of Hyogo and Awaji Island, manufactures "Awaji-gawara", filed for quasi-bankruptcy about two billion yen (Imperial Data Bank) - Yahoo! News
The slump in the housing market and the repeated damage from the earthquake caused "consumers to move away from the tiled roof".
Kinki Ceramics Co., Ltd. (TDB company code: 530173983, capital 9920 million yen, 881-8 Otani, Awaji City, Hyogo Prefecture, representative Kozo Fukuhara, 44 employees) filed for quasi-insolvency to the Kobe District Court on August 30th.
The application agent is Daichi Yoshida (1-10-8 Nishi-Tenma, Kita-ku, Osaka-shi, Osaka Prefecture; Yoshida Daichi Law Office; telephone 06-6365-6038).
Our company is a tile manufacturer that was reorganized into a corporate entity in April 1961, when it was founded in June 1895.
At first, under the trade name of Kinki-gyo Co., Ltd., he started his business as Ibushi-gawara manufacturer, a local industry on Awaji Island, in Nishiawa-cho, Mihara-gun (now Minamiawaji City).
At the same time as succeeding in the absorption-type merger of Kyowa Ceramic Industry Co., Ltd. and the business of Kinki Sangyo Co., Ltd., the head office was moved to the current location after changing to the current trade name.
After that, it opened sales bases in Fukuyama City, Hiroshima City, Oita City, Miyazaki City, etc., and it became the current system, and in the past, it was centered on Ibushi-gawara, but due to changes in consumer needs, Western roof tiles such as flat roof tiles are currently manufactured at a rate of seventy percent, such as Ibushi-gawara.
We built a wide range of business bases mainly in western Japan, such as major house manufacturers, building materials trading companies, and roof construction companies.
He was well-known as one of Awaji Island's top tile manufacturers.
However, due to sluggish housing conditions since the Lehman shock and the repeated damage caused by the Great East Japan Earthquake and the Kumamoto Earthquake, consumer demand has been sluggish due to disengagement from the tiled roof.
In terms of profitability, in addition to the decline in profit margins due to soaring fuel prices, there was a continuous deficit due to the burden of inventories and excessive interest-bearing debt due to capital expenditures, forcing severe financing.
For this reason, financial institutions were requested to ease the conditions for repaying their borrowings, but there was no sign of recovery in business conditions, and the cash flow was gradually tightened.
Unable to support here, he gave up on business continuity, and it became this measure.
The debt is currently under investigation, but is expected to cost about two billion yen.
Prefectural Dinosaur Museum in Katsuyama City, Fukui Prefecture, which is promoting the "industrialization of tourism"
Katsuyama City, Fukui Prefecture, announced on September 25th that it is planning to acquire about twenty-five hundred square meters of land adjacent to the construction site of the roadside station "Dinosaur Valley Katsuyama", which will open next spring.
In order to accelerate the "industrialization of tourism" advocated by the city and the local business community, it aims to attract accommodation-specific hotels and restaurants.
On the same day, the Board of Trustees explained at a special committee on the development of the city's invitation base.
In addition to the opening of the Hokuriku Shinkansen in the spring of 2023, in anticipation of the opening of the entire section of the Chubu Jukan Expressway in the prefecture, the expansion and renovation of the Prefectural Dinosaur Museum (Katsuyama City) and the enhancement of functions, the city would like to promote the shift from a day trip center to a stay-at-home sightseeing, and to attract it around accommodation facilities.
We will acquire a private idle area next to the roadside station construction site east.
Surrounded by the Kuzuryu River, Takinami River, and prefectural roads, it is more than twice the size of a roadside station (approximately twelve thousand square meters).
In the name of the development of an industrial housing complex, the Municipal Land Development Corporation will proceed with land negotiations from this fiscal year, and after the acquisition of the land, the construction of the land will be carried out in the 21st fiscal year.
According to the city, there have already been calls from private businesses such as farmer restaurants and harvesting experience facilities, fast food, and restaurants.
As for accommodation facilities, Sekisui House also revealed that the city has made a name for itself to attract accommodation-specific hotels adjacent to roadside stations planned all over the country.
Sekisui House is scheduled to expand to three locations in Fukui Prefecture, but there is also a possibility that several other cities and towns have expressed their intention to attract them, and there is a possibility of competing.
In addition, the city is planning to build a parking lot.
Some committee members called for speed-up, saying, "The maintenance schedule is slow," and there was also an opinion that there was no place to sleep.
"I would like to appeal the superiority of Katsuyama City with the prefectural dinosaur museum and ski jam Katsuyama at the fore," said Vice-Mayor Yoshio Mizukami, referring to "an industrial housing complex with the theme of tourism," and expressed his intention to make every effort to attract hotels and other facilities.
