[Wakamizu Koshiyama] The circle of "children's cafeteria" that provides free or inexpensive meals to children is spreading throughout the country.
According to a survey by NPOs and others, there are about 3700 locations nationwide this year.
Compared to last year, it has soared by one to six times, and there are twenty-four locations in Fukui Prefecture, an increase of nine places compared to last year.
Why is it a children's cafeteria now?
Makoto Yuasa, the president of a non-profit organization and a social activist, talked about his background and significance at a lecture held in Sakai City.
Due to the declining birthrate and aging population, the area became lonely, and the shopping district also declined.
There was no place for people to meet.
Therefore, "There was a need for an exchange base in the community where people and people were crowded with each other."
Even though it is a children's cafeteria, it is a "place for multi-generational exchanges" in which elderly people also participate.
Not only does it eliminate solitary eating, but it also leads to child-rearing support and the health promotion of the elderly.
Break the cycle of poverty and energize the community.
The most important thing is not to create a child who will be repelled from it.
There are various places from once a month to where meals are served every day.
There are a wide variety of venues, from private homes to temples.
There is an atmosphere where people can do what they want.
The lowness of the threshold is attractive.
However, it is difficult to secure management expenses and staff.
You may have heard of issues such as a safe management system.
However, Mr. Yuasa said, “The values change by interacting with many people” and “Connecting with society is a safety net in the event of a disaster.”
There seems to be a glimmer of hope in a closed society.
