"I hope that South Korea will return to GSOMIA" U.S. Assistant Secretary-General (Sankei Shimbun) - Yahoo! News
U.S. Assistant Minister for U.S. Affairs (South Korea and Japan), Napper, gave an exclusive interview with the Sankei Shimbun at the U.S. ambassador’s residence in Tokyo on the 27th, saying, “I hope that South Korea will reconsider its decision to destroy and come back,” referring to the Japan-South Korea Military Information Comprehensive Agreement (GSOMIA), which expires on November 23rd.
It’s unusual for senior U.S. officials to refer to a reconsideration of the destruction of GSOMIA.
GSOMIA has been concluded between Japan, South Korea, Japan, the U.S., and the United States and South Korea respectively, and is the basis for sharing missile launch information.
In August, South Korea notified that it would cancel the agreement with Japan, and if it expires, the framework to protect military intelligence in Japan and South Korea will collapse.
Napper said, “We can’t effectively respond to the crisis without GSOMIA.”
"There's still plenty of time until it expires," he urged South Korea's return.
“It is the first time in history that China and Russia have conducted a joint flight drill in July over the Sea of Japan,” he said, adding that “it is essential for the U.S., ROK, and Japan, which shares values such as democracy and human rights, to work together in an alliance.”
The recent deterioration in Japan-ROK relations has “hurted U.S. interests.”
“There is no intention of mediating,” he said, adding, “We will play a positive role so that Japan and South Korea can bridge the gap.”
(Yusuke Hirata)
Tokai village criticality accident, twenty years in 30 days Three hundred troubles of nuclear facilities: Hokkaido Shimbun Doshin electronic edition
From FY2000 to FY18, the day after the criticality accident in Tokai Village, Ibaraki Prefecture, which was the first time in Japan's history of nuclear power, there were 336 reports of accidents and breakdowns that occurred at nuclear power-related facilities nationwide, according to an interview with the Nuclear Regulation Authority.
Twenty years have passed since a criticality accident that sounded the alarm about the nuclear industry's safety mythology.
According to the report, the International Scale of Evaluation (INES), which indicates the severity, accounted for about ninety percent of cases that were not evaluated as level zero (not important for safety) and those that were not evaluated, but based on the lessons learned from the accident and the eleven-year-old TEPCO’s Fukushima Daiichi nuclear accident, it is necessary to constantly raise safety awareness.
Mr. Koichi Muneda, who has completed the stories and original drawings of creative picture books and is soliciting donations for publication = September 18th in Echizen City, Fukui Prefecture
Koichi Muneta, 64, an artist from the Echizen City Regional Revitalization Cooperation Team in Fukui Prefecture, has launched a crowdfunding campaign to solicit donations online with the aim of publishing creative picture books inspired by legends in the prefecture in order to promote community development that makes use of picture books.
Mr. Soda, who was born in Hokkaido and started working as a member of the association in April of this year, said, "I want to convey the compassion of the people of Fukui based on their deep faith through picture books."
[Click here for donations] To convey Fukui's heart.
Mr. Soda is active as an artist working on contemporary art with a mathematical motif, but he has also worked as a cooperative team after his 60th anniversary.
After being involved in urban development in Nagano, Kumamoto, and Tochigi, he arrived in Echizen City in April this year.
In connection with the city that produced Riko Kako and Chihiro Iwasaki, we are aiming for community development that makes use of picture books.
Mr. Souda said, "I was surprised by the depth of compassion around me that I knew for the first time when I lived in the city."
What attracted attention was the legend of Awara City, "The face of a bride's ogre".
In contrast to a mother-in-law who can no longer remove the face of an ogre, a kind-hearted wife continues to chant Nenbutsu (Buddhist of Buddha) in order to help her mother-in-law without worrying about being surprised on her face.
"Forgetting hatred and returning it with compassion and kindness. I felt the origin of the faithfulness and kindness of the people of Fukui" (Mr. Soda).
Mr. Soeda, who got a hint for an original picture book, repeated interviews, such as going to Yoshizaki Gobo in Awara City as soon as he arrived, and completed the story "Meoto Oni" featuring a kind-hearted ogre's couple and a boy.
From June, I drew ten scenes with warm watercolours by taking advantage of my main job, and the materials of the picture book were prepared.
In addition, Echizen Washi, a traditional craft that lives in Echizen City, will be used, and the cover of the picture book will be made of washi paper.
It will lead to an opportunity for parents and children to enjoy the texture of washi paper by reading stories.
In preparation for the six-hundred-and-a-half-page publication, the goal for donations is eight
The deadline is until October 25.
Soda said, "Fukui's heart, which has been nurtured under historical Buddhist culture, can be proud all over the country.
I would like to convey the true kindness and compassion that has been handed down to children through picture books."
This is one of the projects in which Fukui Prefecture recognizes eleven projects of regional revitalization challenged by the cooperation teams and businesses in the prefecture, and supports it by utilizing the hometown tax payment.
The Fukui Shimbun provides information, and the Bank of Fukui cooperates in management support.
