New charges for mobile phones Lowering the price with fair competition: Hokkaido Shimbun Doshin electronic edition
With the implementation of the revised Telecommunications Business Act in October, a new pricing plan for mobile phone companies has been released.
However, it generally remained high.
Chief Cabinet Secretary Yoshihide Suga said last summer that "there's room to lower about forty percent," and even though expectations for a price reduction were high, there was no price competition.
A major factor is that Rakuten's full-scale entry has been postponed, and the oligopoly of the three major companies has continued.
For consumers, it is desirable to have an easy-to-use fee structure due to fair competition.
I would like to ask for the efforts of the mobile phone companies.
The revised law prohibits a set discount of terminal fees and communication fees.
This is because, instead of selling the terminal fee, the communication fee was set to be expensive.
SOFTBANK and KDDI (au) have announced plans to make their devices up to half the price.
However, for a hundred days, both companies have a "SIM lock" that can not be used by other companies' lines on their devices.
NTT DOCOMO will pay up to one-thirty a third for subscribers on its own lines.
It seems that all three companies are continuing to "enclose" to prevent switching to other companies.
With regard to SIM locks, the Ministry of Internal Affairs and Communications (MIC) has announced a policy of requiring immediate cancellation.
Businesses also need to respond quickly.
Rakuten's postponement was due to the fact that the development of telecommunications networks, such as base stations, did not proceed as planned.
Since it was expected to be a detonator of the price reduction competition, it is a good appearance with a stiff shoulder.
Did you have a bad view of it?
The timing and price of the full-scale service have not been revealed.
As a company in charge of social infrastructure, I want you to work on your business responsibly.
This change was triggered by Chief Cabinet Secretary Suga's remarks.
"I think that the competition will start from now on. I don't think it's going to happen at all," Suga said.
However, I don't think that "government-made price reduction" distorts the market and promotes healthy competition.
Companies should develop customer-oriented competition before they interfere from the government.
Each company's fee structure is very complex and not easy to compare.
The Consumer Affairs Agency called attention to the fact that there are cases where the burden on the consumer exceeds half the price, even though the device advertises such as "up to half the price".
Companies themselves should try to provide polite and easy-to-understand explanations so as not to create misunderstandings among users.
