The Ministry of Health, Labour and Welfare (MHLW) announced for the first time the names of 424 public hospitals nationwide and public hospitals such as the Red Crash, which it judged to have "poor medical treatment results."
In Fukui Prefecture, the Ministry of Health, Labour and Welfare verified the treatment results of fourteen public and public hospitals ▽ National Hospital Organization Awara Hospital in Awara City ▽ Sakai Municipal Mikuni Hospital ▽ Oda Hospital in Echizen Town ▽ Wakasa Takahama Hospital in Takahama Town was targeted.
⇒ Four hospitals subject to reorganization "standards are strange"
In order to control medical expenses expanding due to the aging population, it is necessary to promote reorganization and integration with hospitals in competing areas, so we decided to take unusual measures.
In October, we requested a full-scale review of the reorganization and integration of the target hospitals, and decided to draw a conclusion by September next year.
There is no coercive power, but there is a reaction of local chiefs and residents due to anxiety that familiar hospitals will disappear.
It was announced at a meeting on the same day.
Twenty-nine or 1 percent of hospitals had relatively few beds.
By prefecture, the proportion was higher, followed by Niigata (53 and 7%), Hokkaido (48 and 6%), Miyagi (47 and 5%), Yamaguchi (46, 7%), and Okayama (43 and 3%).
Fifty-four in Hokkaido was the largest, and Okinawa was the only zero.
The Ministry of Health, Labour and Welfare surveyed public and public hospitals that can respond to the “high acute phase” and the “acute phase” of general surgeries among public and public hospitals.
We analyzed the results of nine medical treatment, such as cancer and emergency medical care, as well as whether the competing hospitals were located within twenty minutes by car, and determined the need to announce the name of the hospital.
Eligible hospitals will be asked to consider abolishing it or moving some medical departments to other hospitals.
Health care costs will soare in twenty-five years, when all of them will be seventy-five or older.
For this reason, the Ministry of Health, Labour and Welfare has set a goal to reduce the number of hospital beds.
Currently, there are a large number of beds in the "high acute phase" and "acute phase" where medical expenses are forcibly arranged by nurses, but there is a shortage for rehabilitation with high needs for the elderly.
In order to cope with the aging population, it is necessary not only to reduce the number of beds, but also to increase the number for rehabilitation.
Regarding the reorganization and consolidation of hospitals, each prefecture has formulated a "regional medical care plan" that determines the number of beds required for twenty-five years.
The entire country is divided into 339 areas, with local governments and medical personnel in consultation, but many of the local residents and chiefs have maintained a cautious stance, making the discussion difficult.
The Ministry of Health, Labour and Welfare hopes to revitalize the discussion by publishing the names of hospitals with poor results.
Assuming Komagadake Eruption, Comprehensive Disaster Prevention Training on Roads, etc. Wide area with the participation of residents, for the first time in eighteen years (Video) : Hokkaido Shimbun Doshin Electronic Version
On September 27th, a comprehensive disaster prevention drill assuming the eruption of Mt.Komagatake (1131m) was held in the forest, Nanae, and Shikabe-cho in Watarishima.
About four hundred people, including officials and residents of twenty agencies, including roads and three towns, participated.
This is the first time that the Hokkaido Komagatake Volcano Disaster Prevention Council has drawn up an evacuation plan that includes evacuation routes for residents in August.
This is the first time in eighteen years that a wide-area training for residents has participated.
It was assumed that Mt. Komagadake had erupted at 8:45 a.m. in the morning, and that a muddy stream had reached the town at the foot of the mountain.
When the eruption warning level was raised to five (evacuation), evacuation shelters were opened in the forests and Nanae towns, and residents moved by buses prepared by each town with buses and roadside police vehicles.
