Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries (Gifu Shimbun Web) - Yahoo! News
The Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries on the 27th released a draft revision of the epidemic prevention guidelines that will enable preventive vaccination of domestic pigs, which are not currently recognized, in order to prevent the spread of the spread of the livestock infectious disease "pork cholera".
Nine prefectures, including Gifu and Aichi prefectures, have been confirmed to be infected with pigs and wild boars.
The movement of vaccinated live pigs and fertilized eggs will be restricted in the region, but the circulation of meat and processed products is permitted outside the region.
Through public comments to the general public (opinion solicitation), we aim to complete the procedure as early as October.
By using the vaccine and allowing extraterrestrial distribution such as meat, it will no longer become a "purity country" that has been certified by international organizations, and it may affect the export of pork.
Since the outbreak in September last year, Japan’s status as a clean country has been temporarily suspended.
Based on the opinions of experts, the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries has established a "vaccination recommended area" in areas where there is a high risk of infection from wild boars to pigs.
The target prefecture will create a "vaccination program" that summarizes the scope, timing, number of heads, how to proceed, etc.
The other seven prefectures listed as the recommended regional plans for vaccination are Saitama, Toyama, Ishikawa, Fukui, Nagano, Mie, and Shiga.
When the guidelines are revised, the situation will be reviewed and decided again.
In principle, the movement of vaccinated pigs, semen, fertilized eggs, carcasses, excrement, etc. will be restricted to the region.
With regard to meat and processed products, due to the cost of distribution restrictions and the impact on farmers, it will not be targeted, and will effectively allow distribution outside the region.
In order to prevent the spread of the virus, we will thoroughly dispose of food waste.
We will appeal for the safety of vaccinated pigs, etc., and work to prevent reputational damage.
The proposed amendments to the guidelines were compiled at a meeting of experts held on the same day and confirmed at a meeting at a meeting of the Anti-epidemic Countermeasures Headquarters.
In the future, opinion inquiries and public comments to prefectures will be conducted until October 7th, and new epidemic prevention guidelines will be implemented.
