How to prevent dementia and keep it to the degree of "forgotten things"?
Interview / Text / Yo Watanabe
Major pharmaceutical companies and university hospitals are struggling with their research and development.
However, the relationship between dementia and lifestyle is being elucidated, and it has become clear that there is a possibility that it can be kept to an extremely mild illness or forgetfulness.
We spoke to Professor Shunsuke Otsuki of the Stroke Center at Kinki University Hospital.
Dementia will not disappear.
Dementia prevention or treatment is expected, but what is the current situation?
Ōtsuki From mild to severe, depending on the degree, everyone may have dementia as they age.
However, it is still expected to take more than two decades for new dementia drugs to be developed.
Donepezil, which is often used in Japan, is no longer covered by insurance in some overseas countries, and we can’t expect a noticeable healing effect.
On the other hand, deaths from infectious diseases and accidents have decreased, and cancer deaths have also decreased due to remarkable advances in treatments such as anti-cancer drugs, and people can live longer.
Therefore, it is also true that the number of patients with dementia is increasing.
It is not something that can be solved only by yourself and your family, and it is necessary to create an environment where society as a whole can live with peace of mind in
How to “forgotten”
Is there people who are prone to dementia and people who are difficult to become?
Ōtsuki Even if dementia is inevitable, there is a possibility of suppressing symptoms as much as possible and limiting it to “forgotten things”.
Dementia is considered to be one of the lifestyle diseases, and there is a tendency for people who are easy to become.
First of all, people with metabolic syndrome tend to be prone to dementia.
Dementia is seen as a lifestyle disease.
It is a combination of obesity, hypertension, diabetes, hypercholesterolemia.
The lack of economic power will also have an impact.
For example, in the U.S., there are areas where food vouchers are distributed to the poor, but the foods that can be obtained with food vouchers are frozen hamburgers, pizzas and chicken that can be stored.
You can't use gas or electricity, and people who live in an environment where they can't cook in the first place can use it.
As a result, it becomes a diet with less vegetables and proteins, and it becomes a lifestyle that is biased towards lipids and carbohydrates.
Before long, you will be in poverty, but you will become obese, and the only thing you have to do is watch TV, and your body will be disabled.
A similar situation can occur in Japan.
Others who don’t think or don’t work tend to have dementia.
In other words, in order to prevent dementia, it is necessary to read books and think about things on a daily basis, which is not metabolic.
